sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch12-14> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Species that become naturalised in new countries are generally not closely allied to the native inhabitants but are very distinct, often belonging to genera absent from the region, whereas closely allied species equally well fitted to their places tend to keep separate.";
    dc:type "empirical-generalization";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Naturalised species are usually not closely allied to the natives they join";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1st ed., London: John Murray, 1859), Chapter XII, “Geographical Distribution, continued”; section “On the relation of the inhabitants of islands to those of the nearest mainland”. Scope: naturalised (introduced) plants and animals"
}
